13.3  /proc and /sys

TL;DR
  - Neither is on disk: the kernel makes up their content when you read it.
  - /proc: one folder per process (/proc/1234), plus system-wide files such as
    /proc/meminfo and /proc/cpuinfo.
  - /sys: devices and drivers, as a tree (/sys/block, /sys/class/net).
  - Some files can be written to change kernel settings live. That needs root,
    and the change is lost at reboot.

      cat /proc/self/status     # the process reading it: cat itself
      ls /sys/class/net         # your network interfaces

Pitfalls
  - Most /proc files show a size of 0 in ls -l: their content only exists when
    read. Use cat, not the size.
  - Values are a snapshot: read twice, get two answers.
  - /proc/sys holds what sysctl changes: sysctl vm.swappiness reads
    /proc/sys/vm/swappiness. Persistent settings go in /etc/sysctl.d/.
  - A PID is reused after its process exits: /proc/1234 can be a different
    program a minute later.

Deep dive

  ▾ Why two of them?

    /proc came first. Its idea, processes as files, comes from Unix research
    systems of the 1980s. On Linux it grew into a place for all kinds of kernel
    information, with no rules on what goes where.

    /sys (sysfs) arrived with kernel 2.6 and a stricter design: one value per
    file, arranged by the kernel's model of devices and drivers. New device
    information goes to /sys; /proc keeps processes and its historical files.

  ▾ How does idk read your machine?

    Mostly from these two trees: memory from /proc/meminfo, processes from
    /proc/PID/stat, disks from /sys/block, network interfaces from
    /sys/class/net. Reading files needs no root and runs no other program, which
    is why idk can stay read-only.

Related
  Commands  cat, sysctl
  Paths     /proc, /sys, /proc/sys
  See also  idk 13.1 The hierarchy in 2 minutes
